  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA cold front was bisecting southeast Kansas this morning with a \nconsiderable amount of post-frontal stratus impacting much of \nnorthern and portions of central Kansas. This is expected to keep \ntemperatures in the 70s for most areas behind the front with some \nmodest low level caa. Meanwhile, some breaks in the clouds will \nallow highs to climb to near 90 in the far southeast corner of KS \nearly in the period with steady or falling temperatures anticipated. \nSome showers and thunderstorms are expected to develop in the \nbuoyant and weakly capped airmass along and ahead of the front this \nafternoon while the focus gradually shifts south and east of the \narea as we move into the evening and overnight hours. A moist \nupslope flow regime over the Central High Plains is expected to \nresult in a complex of showers and storms developing this evening. \nThis activity is expected to drift south and east across western \nKansas possibly clipping our central KS counties during the \novernight hours before diminishing, although higher probabilities \nare expected to remain further west.\n\nMon-Wed...A stable post-frontal regime is anticipated through the \nperiod with light northeast winds. The mid/upper ridge is progged to \nbuild over the stable shallow airmass with minimal impacts to \nsensible weather. Highs are expected to range from 75-80 through the \nperiod with lows mostly in the 50s, which is near climate normals \nfor late Sep.\n\nBy Wed night, we may see some increase in shower activity as rich \nmoisture wraps around the mid/upper ridge centered over the lower \nMississippi Valley area. PWATs are progged to rise above 1.5\" as \nmore subtle features rotate around this feature. This moist plume is \nprogged to remain across the Central and Southern Plains into the \nupcoming weekend with multiple opportunities for beneficial rainfall \nthrough the period. Temperatures are expected to moderate by this \nweekend with above average values returning to the area Sat-Sun. The \nthreat for a few strong or severe storms may be hard to rule out \nover the upcoming weekend.",
